Add @olive oil{2%tbsp} to a large, heavy-bottomed #pot{} over medium-high heat. Use paper towel to pat the @pork shoulder{2.5%lbs} as dry as possible. Generously season the @pork shoulder{} with @kosher salt{2%tsp} and @ground black pepper{2%tsp}. Once the oil in the pot shimmers, carefully add in the seasoned @pork shoulder{}. Work in batches, as necessary, to avoid overcrowding the pot (which prevents browning). Cook for ~{4-5%minutes} per side, until nicely browned. Transfer the browned @pork shoulder{} to a plate and set aside.

If needed, add the remaining @olive oil{1%tbsp} to the same pot used in Step 1 and reduce heat to medium. Once hot, add in the soffritto (@carrots{3}(peeled and diced), @onion{1}(finely diced), and @celery{1}(finely diced)), seasoning with @kosher salt{0.5%tsp} and @ground black pepper{0.5%tsp} and stirring to combine. Cook, stirring occasionally, until deeply browned, about ~{15-20%minutes}.

Add the @garlic{8%cloves}(finely chopped) to the pot with the soffritto. Stirring constantly, cook until fragrant, about ~{1-2%minutes}. Add the @tomato paste{2%tbsp} to the pot, stirring to coat the soffritto. Cook for ~{2-3%minutes}, until browned.

Increasing the heat to medium-high, pour the @dry white wine{1%cup} into the pot. Stir constantly, using a wooden spoon to scrape up any browned bits that may have formed at the bottom of the pot. Cook for ~{4-5%minutes}, until the wine is almost completely absorbed into the soffritto.

Tie the @poultry herb blend{1}(or approx. 2 sprigs fresh rosemary, 4 sprigs fresh sage leaves and 12 sprigs fresh thyme) together using kitchen twine (or finely chop if you do not have twine), then add to the pot with the @bay leaves{2}. Add the @crushed tomatoes{28%oz can}, @water{2%cups} (or low-sodium chicken broth or stock), and browned @pork shoulder{} from Step 1. Stir to combine. Bring the mixture to a boil. Reduce heat to maintain a gentle simmer. Cover and cook, stirring occasionally, for ~{2.5-3%hours}, or until the @pork shoulder{} is fall-apart tender. If the ragu begins to reduce too much (i.e. it loses too much of its liquid too quickly), feel free to add in a splash more @water{} and/or reduce the heat further.

Carefully transfer the @pork shoulder{} to a plate or cutting board. At this point, you can remove and discard the spent herbs and @bay leaves{} from the pot, as well. Use tongs or 2 forks to shred the @pork shoulder{} into bite-sized pieces. Return the shredded @pork shoulder{} to the pot with the ragu. Stir to combine. At this point, you can cool and store for later use (see Recipe Notes for storage and freezing directions), or proceed with making the pork ragu pappardelle (below).

Bring a large #pot{} of salted @water{} to a boil. Add the @pappardelle{20-30%oz} and cook, stirring occasionally, until the pasta is cooked to al dente according to package directions. Carefully dip a liquid measuring cup into the pot, reserving about @water{1%cup} of the starchy pasta water, and set aside. Carefully drain the @pappardelle{} – do NOT rinse it!

Meanwhile, as the pasta boils, bring the braised pork ragu up to a simmer. Stir in the @heavy cream{0.5%cup} and @parmesan{heaping 0.5%cup}. Continue to simmer over low heat, stirring occasionally.

Add the cooked @pappardelle{} to the pot with the braised pork ragu sauce, tossing to coat. The pork ragu should evenly coat the pasta. Add in some of the reserved pasta water if the ragu needs to loosen up a little; add in an extra handful of @parmesan{} if it needs to tighten up a little. Cook over medium heat for ~{1-2%minutes}, allowing the pasta to meld with and absorb some of the ragu.

Portion the pork ragu pappardelle into individual pasta bowls, topping with additional @parmesan{}(grated), finely chopped fresh herbs, and/or crushed red pepper as desired. Serve immediately. Enjoy!